## 2. Inventory policy models

### Intended use

Recommend rotable floats, consumable and expendable reorder points, and hub-versus-outstation positioning that trade spares capital against expected material-related downtime. Show the trade-off as a frontier, explain each unit of stock, and test recommendations in simulation before presenting them.

### Models

| Component | Method |
|---|---|
| {{RESULT:v21.name.P0}} | Deliberately simplified starting point on supplier quotes (not a model of a competent airline): Poisson protection {{RESULT:config.p0_protection}} on quoted data, held at AUH, a line-station unit only for D0 parts with at least {{RESULT:config.p0_line_min}} quoted removals a year at that station |
| Rotable float | Palm's theorem: pipeline ~ Poisson(λ · E[T]); EBO and fill P(X ≤ S − 1) by stock level; λ from the reliability-exposure posterior |
| Reorder points | Lead-time demand quantile at the rectification-window target (D0 0.97, D3 0.92, D10 0.85; ASSUMED); S = s + EOQ |
| Positioning: {{RESULT:v21.name.P2}} | Pooled hub float on network demand; an outstation unit is added while its avoided miss cost (v2 AOG routes, hourly rate, cost per event) per AED of unit cost is at least the 20% carrying rate. METRIC-inspired, not full METRIC |
| Readiness Frontier (how much readiness each extra dirham buys); {{RESULT:v21.name.I125}} is one point on it | Greedy marginal allocation within stated guardrails (G1 99.5% fill cap per stocking point, G2 one unit at low-rate outstations). A heuristic |
| Referee | Policy-agnostic event engine with the v2 AOG routes, paired seeds, frozen parameters |

